#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82ffdd7b-a690-51d0-951c-1c4aac869f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220418} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a7f232-3377-5311-a581-3245bc51ac4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220419} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c39954-602f-590b-a00b-579f1a998b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220420} {:answer/id #uuid "07834139-c8de-5a21-b983-beb447afa412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220421} {:answer/id #uuid "574a5d3b-1d23-59c0-878d-62a8505ac969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7c39954-602f-590b-a00b-579f1a998b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220420} :answer-field/id #uuid "15777d08-b0bb-5204-8540-59ff6c2d717b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f2ba467-6ea9-5201-b847-1a519dc1ed8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333576" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3}$\n- $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{3}=9(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371d5d0d-0a4c-515c-b2db-00ad19b0f3f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338683} {:answer/id #uuid "86b8bfd6-8cdf-5ba6-9701-86a2fc8096ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338684} {:answer/id #uuid "70b683ac-d889-5751-928a-2e3244869cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338685} {:answer/id #uuid "304d8479-26b9-532c-8eb4-750823e6c56c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338686} {:answer/id #uuid "b3588f0c-aad1-5915-a6f2-6c86ce681172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "371d5d0d-0a4c-515c-b2db-00ad19b0f3f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338683} :answer-field/id #uuid "afaea038-a4d6-5c43-aaaf-06cbe4b5e728" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0158779-3bb5-5926-932e-57d4963e6b20" :question/math-academy-id "q-334648" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.35,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.35)=0.75.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.005.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.35$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.35}{0.10}=3.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(3.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.75$ to $0.005.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.75}{0.005}=150.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.5)^{p+1} & ≈150 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3.5}⁡(150) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.999… \\\\ p & ≈2.999….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method By Comparing LTE for Different Step Sizes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40b938c1-d0a2-56e9-97a0-b28d20fdd30c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.057" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338690} {:answer/id #uuid "5684bcf7-4718-5eb3-8a21-4853389798ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.420" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338691} {:answer/id #uuid "07522058-7c46-5427-85c3-dc577aea7ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338692} {:answer/id #uuid "08c848bb-653a-533a-85d4-dfa289c846fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338693} {:answer/id #uuid "c054062f-958a-5f8b-8d2c-b7b24a5c2a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5684bcf7-4718-5eb3-8a21-4853389798ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.420" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338691} :answer-field/id #uuid "c373e60e-d399-5c20-a016-14da2f05a5c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338689} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf8cf7b2-ccf8-5363-8310-8feb55a2d287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.057" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338696} {:answer/id #uuid "c3939c48-bbd2-51e4-b773-f494b4d4fbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338697} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7ac5ec-c4f5-55ee-9f6a-3eb0bfbedc87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.420" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338698} {:answer/id #uuid "24e58960-6c3a-589d-bdbc-29bd9882737d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338699} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3529f4-7b1e-58e1-885d-53a0a5692460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf8cf7b2-ccf8-5363-8310-8feb55a2d287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.057" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338696} :answer-field/id #uuid "57c05250-0e27-51e7-9ad0-460eea378b0f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338695} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15346b38-82c5-5175-9983-986f19f2b702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.361" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338702} {:answer/id #uuid "eb190b94-153e-540b-b093-8d0a5facec2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.603" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338703} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a001ed-13cd-505f-bd0b-d89cfa4cd562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.957" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338704} {:answer/id #uuid "95c22070-1d88-5664-bec3-f3ae2645b889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.323" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338705} {:answer/id #uuid "2b31bbd8-ecb4-5671-bd1d-8fe706d635f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95c22070-1d88-5664-bec3-f3ae2645b889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.323" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338705} :answer-field/id #uuid "300b39fc-7b95-554d-8671-63f747e0950f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338701} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ac49277-9642-5c6b-9596-69d35d3f7602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338708} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c8fd57-3e21-562a-8bbb-f728f7447f5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338709} {:answer/id #uuid "158c09d9-e845-5d1d-8f11-89ff8f10efe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338710} {:answer/id #uuid "7ace811c-6d6b-5be9-9501-bbf7d47faeb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338711} {:answer/id #uuid "002805ea-0950-5056-aa0b-779c8cfb3fdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ace811c-6d6b-5be9-9501-bbf7d47faeb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338711} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f04376b-d047-53db-b69d-099eb90dfe96" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "191ac454-645f-542b-be97-450b5f3d1a65" :question/math-academy-id "q-334710" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}-(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.6) & =6(0.6)^{5}-(0.6)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.077\\,76-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.466\\,56-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.419\\,904 \\\\  & ≈0.420.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.4) & =6(0.4)^{5}-(0.4)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.010\\,24-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.061\\,44-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.057\\,344 \\\\  & ≈0.057.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.4.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.4}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={1.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.419\\,904$ to $0.057\\,344.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.419\\,904}{0.057\\,344}=7.322\\,54…≈7.323.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1.5}^{p+1} & ≈7.322\\,54… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{1.5}⁡(7.322\\,54…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.910… \\\\ p & ≈3.910….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}-(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11217aba-7942-5c3a-84cf-37f22aa209e3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Order of a Numerical Method Given the GTE or LTE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a49d378a-b07f-5e09-85f6-5fcc96f7fedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338715} {:answer/id #uuid "287d93c6-0cdf-5acd-b44a-b5d41b24e85c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338716} {:answer/id #uuid "74e26eb5-2461-52e6-9366-65648ba85ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338717} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4ccffc-c54c-54c0-b429-83f623aa78b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338718} {:answer/id #uuid "0051b7f1-7fda-58b6-ae60-9ef229dd4508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c4ccffc-c54c-54c0-b429-83f623aa78b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338718} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8421b7e-5b2a-5070-98d2-12fb201b0d03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49d42f68-ce62-5114-8630-2000eca229d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-334761" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7}$\n- $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.5(Δx)^{4}=0.5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4c7f962-87af-53af-8167-d24ade127366" :knowledge-point/title "Making Inferences Given the Order of a Numerical Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd72683-d4b7-5963-a523-5ad25dcde25f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bd72683-d4b7-5963-a523-5ad25dcde25f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338722} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b88f266-70c0-5413-b79d-d7e4231774ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4057e0b-c56a-5d6e-a19f-cf1eace4c3c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-334763" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.2$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.2)≈0.064.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.2$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.2}{0.1}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.1)≈\\frac{0.064}{8}=0.008.\n$$"}]}
